  "historical_output_text": " Photosynthesis is a fundamental biological process used by plants to convert light energy into chemical energy stored in sugar molecules. The central idea is that plants capture sunlight, use it to split water molecules, and combine the resulting hydrogen with carbon dioxide to produce glucose and oxygen.\n\nHere is a simplified sequence of how photosynthesis works:\n\n1. Light absorption: Chlorophyll and other pigments in the chloroplasts of plant cells absorb light energy, mostly from the red and blue ends of the visible spectrum.\n\n2. Water splitting: Using the absorbed energy, the plant splits water molecules (H2O) into oxygen (O2), protons (H+), and electrons. This occurs in the thylakoid membranes.\n\n3. Electron transport: The electrons are passed through a series of proteins and molecules, creating a proton gradient and ATP (energy) production.\n\n4. Carbon fixation",
